1. What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned dashboard that shows the state of a scientific field. The state of a field is described by how many claims are contested, how many have been replicated, how many have been retracted, how many have been superseded, and how those numbers change over time. The Meta-Science Dashboard aggregates the data from the other apps in the suite, computes the field-level metrics, and presents them as a live dashboard. It is the top-level view of the suite, the place where the whole system's output is summarised.

It reuses:

the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23)

the Replication Tracker (App 26)

the Anomaly Register (App 25)

the Medical Claim Verifier (App 40)

the Psychological Effect Registry (App 41)

the Climate Attribution Registry (App 42)

the Historical Claim Registry (App 38)

the Mathematical Conjecture Registry (App 44)

the Claim Lifecycle Tracker (App 45)

the Evidence Aggregator (App 46)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the PROV-O Exporter (App 3)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for funders, journal editors, meta-scientists, science policymakers, and anyone who wants to see the state of a field.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept a field selection from the user.

Aggregate the data from the other apps for the selected field.

Compute the field-level metrics: claims contested, claims replicated, claims retracted, claims superseded, claims open.

Compute the trends: how the metrics change over time.

Compute the comparison: how the field compares to other fields.

Compute the hotspots: which claims have the most activity.

Compute the cold spots: which claims have the least activity.

Compute the funding impact: how much funding has gone to which claims.

Compute the time-to-resolution: how long claims take to resolve.

Compute the replication rate: what fraction of claims have been replicated.

Compute the retraction rate: what fraction of claims have been retracted.

Compute the evidence strength distribution: how the evidence strength is distributed across claims.

Record the dashboard state: the field, the time period, the metrics, the trends.

Record the data sources: which apps contributed which data.

Record the provenance: the data collection date, the aggregation tool version.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by dashboard ID, field, time period, metric, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the dashboard description, the metrics, and the notes.

Support exporting a dashboard as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing a dashboard from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support the field overview: a visualisation of the field's metrics.

Support the trend view: a visualisation of how the metrics change over time.

Support the comparison view: a visualisation that compares the field to other fields.

Support the hotspot view: a visualisation of the claims with the most activity.

Support the cold spot view: a visualisation of the claims with the least activity.

Support the funding view: a visualisation of the funding impact.

Support the time-to-resolution view: a visualisation of the time-to-resolution distribution.

Support the evidence strength view: a visualisation of the evidence strength distribution.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical dashboard in under 500 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^7 records across the suite.

Versioned: the system records the STAG schema version, the Null-Model Protocol schema version, the meta-science schema version, and the system version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

